## State machine (implement in WelcomePortal)

```
runChannel == UNDECIDED (first launch)
  ├─ cold push URL present? → STREAM with that URL (highest priority)
  ├─ no internet → OfflinePortal ON THE FIRST FRAME. Nothing started, nothing
  │    persisted; the link returning restarts this whole router. NEVER the game
  │    — an install that came through a link must still reach the WebView.
  ├─ has internet:
  │    ├─ start AppsFlyer NOW, not earlier (see kotlin_launch_flow.mdc §1–2)
  │    ├─ await conversion (30s) and deferred deep link (5s) in parallel
  │    ├─ build POST body (attribution + deep link + device + push + firebase)
  │    ├─ ReachDispatch.fetchChannel(body)
  │    ├─ ok+url → save url+expires, runChannel=STREAM → AlertPortal?→StreamPortal
  │    └─ else   → game; persist NATIVE only if the endpoint answered AND the
  │               attribution was non-empty, otherwise leave UNDECIDED

runChannel == STREAM (returning, was WebView)
  ├─ cold push URL → StreamPortal (highest priority, one-time)
  ├─ no internet → OfflinePortal (with savedUrl if any)
  ├─ start AppsFlyer, attribution (10s) → fetchChannel
  │    ├─ ok+url → StreamPortal(new url) + persist
  │    ├─ fail + savedUrl(not expired) → StreamPortal(savedUrl)
  │    └─ fail + no saved → OfflinePortal

runChannel == NATIVE (returning, was game)
  └─ native game, ALWAYS, no network needed, AppsFlyer not started
```

Key invariant: **once the backend says native, the user stays native forever**
(don't re-query, don't show WebView on later visits). The backend owns the
conversion decision, not the client.

The counter-invariant, equally hard: **a "no" is only recorded when a server
actually said it, to a request that actually carried attribution.** A transport
failure or an empty conversion map is not a verdict — open the game for that
run and leave the channel UNDECIDED. Full reasoning and code in
`kotlin_launch_flow.mdc` §5.

Everything about *when* each step may run — the two-stroke AppsFlyer start, the
offline first launch, the timeouts, and the log lines that prove it works —
lives in **`kotlin_launch_flow.mdc`**. Read it before writing the router.

